From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mihawk <163614687+mihawk-99@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 23:48:38 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] mfreadwrite: Signal decoder stream changes against the type
 downstream holds.

GE-Proton's media rework (patches 2001-2062) made two choices that together
hide a decoder's new frame size from the consumer. Its source reader signals
MF_SOURCE_READERF_CURRENTMEDIATYPECHANGED only when the transform's new output
type differs from the old one (2002), and its decoder replaces its current
output type itself when it raises MF_E_TRANSFORM_STREAM_CHANGE (2018). The
reader then compares the new type with an already updated one, finds them
equal, and never tells the consumer.

A 1920x1080 H.264 clip read as NV12 shows it: winedmo's decoder announces the
coded 1920x1088 frame with a 1920x1080 aperture and writes 3133440-byte
buffers, while IMFSourceReader_GetCurrentMediaType keeps reporting 1920x1080
with no aperture, so a consumer that places the chroma plane after 1080 rows
reads it 8 rows early (tests/mf-video-probe.c on GE-Proton11-7's own Linux
build: luma bit-exact, chroma differs; chroma after 1088 rows bit-exact).

Compare the new output type with what downstream holds instead: the stream's
current type for the last transform, the next transform's input type
otherwise.
---
 dlls/mfreadwrite/reader.c | 24 +++++++++++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/dlls/mfreadwrite/reader.c b/dlls/mfreadwrite/reader.c
index 16f5736..3b55129 100644
--- a/dlls/mfreadwrite/reader.c
+++ b/dlls/mfreadwrite/reader.c
@@ -1241,8 +1241,11 @@ static HRESULT set_matching_transform_output_type(IMFTransform *transform, IMFMe
     return hr;
 }
 
-/* update the transform output type while keeping subtype which matches the old output type */
-static HRESULT transform_entry_update_output_type(struct transform_entry *entry, IMFMediaType *old_output_type)
+/* update the transform output type while keeping subtype which matches the old output type; the change is signalled
+ * when the new type differs from what downstream holds (the stream's current type or the next transform's input type),
+ * or from the old output type when downstream is not given */
+static HRESULT transform_entry_update_output_type(struct transform_entry *entry, IMFMediaType *old_output_type,
+        IMFMediaType *downstream_type)
 {
     IMFMediaType *new_output_type = NULL;
     DWORD flags = 0;
@@ -1258,7 +1261,8 @@ static HRESULT transform_entry_update_output_type(struct transform_entry *entry,
      * and the effective consumer-visible format did not change. */
     {
         HRESULT get_hr = IMFTransform_GetOutputCurrentType(entry->transform, 0, &new_output_type);
-        HRESULT eq_hr = new_output_type ? IMFMediaType_IsEqual(old_output_type, new_output_type, &flags) : E_FAIL;
+        HRESULT eq_hr = new_output_type ? IMFMediaType_IsEqual(downstream_type ? downstream_type : old_output_type,
+                new_output_type, &flags) : E_FAIL;
 
         if (FAILED(get_hr) || FAILED(eq_hr) || !(flags & MF_MEDIATYPE_EQUAL_FORMAT_DATA))
             entry->pending_flags |= MF_SOURCE_READERF_CURRENTMEDIATYPECHANGED;
@@ -1283,7 +1287,7 @@ static HRESULT transform_entry_update_input_type(struct transform_entry *entry,
 
     /* check if transform output type is still valid or if we need to update it as well */
     if (FAILED(hr = IMFTransform_GetOutputCurrentType(entry->transform, 0, &new_output_type)))
-        hr = transform_entry_update_output_type(entry, old_output_type);
+        hr = transform_entry_update_output_type(entry, old_output_type, NULL);
     else
         IMFMediaType_Release(new_output_type);
 
@@ -1400,7 +1404,17 @@ static HRESULT source_reader_pull_transform_samples(struct source_reader *reader
 
         if (hr == MF_E_TRANSFORM_STREAM_CHANGE && SUCCEEDED(hr = IMFTransform_GetOutputCurrentType(entry->transform, 0, &media_type)))
         {
-            hr = transform_entry_update_output_type(entry, media_type);
+            /* A decoder may already report its new type as current (winedmo's does), so compare with what
+             * downstream was given, or a new frame size never reaches the consumer. */
+            IMFMediaType *downstream_type = NULL;
+
+            if (!next)
+                IMFMediaType_AddRef((downstream_type = stream->current));
+            else if (FAILED(IMFTransform_GetInputCurrentType(next->transform, 0, &downstream_type)))
+                downstream_type = NULL;
+            hr = transform_entry_update_output_type(entry, media_type, downstream_type);
+            if (downstream_type)
+                IMFMediaType_Release(downstream_type);
             IMFMediaType_Release(media_type);
 
             if (SUCCEEDED(hr))
-- 
2.56.0

